  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced national capacity to develop and implement a gender sensitive policy and strategies to promote employment creation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Africa is undergoing a structural shift driven by decarbonisation and the transition to a low-carbon economy, reshaping labour demand in carbon-intensive and emerging green sectors. However, significant skills mismatches, limited reskilling systems, and weak institutional readiness constrain the ability of workers, youth, and training providers to adapt. In collaboration with social partners and skills development stakeholders in the Just Energy Transition (JET), support focuses on strengthening inclusive social dialogue on reskilling and upskilling for workers most at risk, targeting Mpumalanga and Eastern Cape due to their vulnerability to JET-related labour market disruption and emerging green economy opportunities. Working with industry, business chambers, local government, and skills development stakeholders, support is also provided to upskill TVET trainers in JET-related occupations and expand targeted reskilling and upskilling opportunities for youth, vulnerable workers, and communities to participate in emerging green economy opportunities.  The Government of South Africa has declared 2026 the Year of Putting Young People to Work, placing youth employment at the centre of its national agenda following the 2026 State of the Nation Address.  In support of this national priority and working in collaboration with relevant agencies in the UN, the ILO is conducting a mapping and impact assessment of existing youth employment initiatives to generate evidence-based insights that will inform dialogue for a forward-looking youth programme of action.  The targeted beneficiaries are the unemployed youth, women, and groups in situations of vulnerability.</narrative>
  </description>
